<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Cat Says 'Meow'.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write simple words with model guidance. interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can draw a simple picture and label it with one or two familiar words with teacher support (e.g., 'cat' or 'dog'). Can engage in interactive labeling activities by labeling pictures of animals and their sounds (e.g., write 'cow' and 'moo' under a picture of a cow). Can label drawings of animals created by peers with correct information (e.g., add 'fish' next to a peer's drawing of a fish).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "What's that sound?" activity. The teacher plays animal sounds (e.g., 'meow', 'woof', 'quack') or mimics them. Students identify the animal and mimic the sound. The teacher shows corresponding flashcards. "What sound is this?" "It's a cat! The cat says meow!" (Grammar: WH-questions, Present simple, Pragmatic: Mimicking sounds). Focus on /m/ in 'meow' and /r/ in 'rooster'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a cat. "This is a cat. The cat says meow." The teacher then writes "cat" and "meow" on the board, sounding out the letters clearly. "C-A-T, cat." "M-E-O-W, meow." The teacher demonstrates proper letter formation, emphasizing the initial letters 'C' and 'M'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the written word 'cat') "Is this 'dog'?" (Expected answer: "No, cat!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to 'meow' next to a cow picture) "Does the cow say 'meow'?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful flashcards of animals (cat, dog, rooster, cow, duck, pig, horse, fish, sheep, bird) with a simple sentence strip below each, initially blank or with tracing lines. A simple "Animal Sound Book" (large format, with a picture on one page and a sentence like "The [animal] says [sound]" on the facing page).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the "Animal Sound Book." Page by page, the teacher shows an animal picture (e.g., a cow). "This is a cow." Then, the teacher reveals the sentence: "The cow says moo." The teacher writes 'cow' and 'moo' on the board next to the picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choral repeat the animal name and sound. "The cow says moo." (Grammar: Present simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces new letters P, Q, R by showing pictures of animals starting with those letters (e.g., Pig, Quack/Duck, Rooster), writing the letters and words clearly on the board, and having students recognize them. "P for Pig!" "Q for Quack!" "R for Rooster!"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying and describing different animals based on their sounds. (Sociolinguistic: Describing animal sounds, Pragmatic: Recognizing sounds).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays several flashcards and says a sound (e.g., "Woof!"). Students point to the corresponding animal (dog).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of an animal (e.g., a pig) and asks, "What sound does the pig say?" Students answer, "Oink!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing familiar animal names (cat, dog, cow, pig, duck, rooster) and their sounds (meow, bark, moo, oink, quack). (Grammar: Present simple; Vocabulary: nouns, animal_sounds; Phonemic awareness: C, D, P, Q, R).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Tracing Animal Words:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with pictures of animals (e.g., cat, dog, cow) and dotted lines below each picture for the animal's name and sound (e.g., "cat", "meow"). Students use pencils to trace the words. The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance on letter formation and pronunciation of the words as students trace. (Skills: Writing, Phonemic awareness: C, D, M, R).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Match and Label:</b> The teacher gives each student a worksheet with animal pictures and a word bank of animal names and sounds (e.g., 'dog', 'bark', 'pig', 'oink'). Students are instructed to draw a line from each word to the correct picture, then copy the word next to the picture. For Kinder, the teacher may pre-fill some letters, or provide small cut-out words for them to paste. "This is a pig. Find 'pig' and write it." (Skills: Writing, Vocabulary recognition).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Interactive Labeling Circle:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher places large pictures of animals (e.g., a rooster, a duck) in the center. The teacher writes animal names and sounds on separate small cards. One student picks a card (e.g., 'rooster'), reads it (with teacher help), and places it next to the correct animal picture. Then, another student picks a sound card (e.g., 'oink') and places it next to the pig. This continues, reinforcing the connection between the written word, the animal, and its sound. "That's a rooster!" (Exclamations).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Drawing and Labeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My Animal, Your Label!"</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a blank sheet of paper and crayons. The teacher instructs them to "Draw your favorite animal!" (e.g., 'cat', 'dog', 'fish').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once drawings are complete, students exchange drawings with a partner.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a word bank on the board with written animal names and their sounds (e.g., "cat - meow", "dog - bark", "fish - swim"). Students (with teacher support and peer help) try to label their partner's drawing with the correct animal name and sound, by copying from the board or tracing pre-written labels provided by the teacher. "What sound is this?" (pointing to a dog picture). "The dog says bark."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering assistance, checking for correct labels, and encouraging students. (Skills: Writing, Peer collaboration, Vocabulary application).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in producing written labels, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students are applying their developing writing skills to label drawings of animals and their sounds, connecting directly to the theme "The Cat Says 'Meow'." and scenario "What's that Sound?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Animal, Your Label!" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students hand in their labeled peer drawings. The teacher observes if students can draw a simple picture and label it with one or two familiar words (e.g., 'cat' or 'dog') with support, and if they engaged in interactive labeling activities by labeling pictures of animals and their sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of Labels: Are the written animal names and sounds legible?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctness: Do the labels match the animal drawings? (e.g., 'cat' for a cat picture, 'meow' for its sound).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort and Participation: Did the student attempt to write/trace and engage with the peer's drawing?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary: Did the students try to use words like 'cat', 'dog', 'rooster', 'meow', 'bark', 'quack'?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "Thumbs Up" if they feel they can write some animal names, and "Thumbs Down" if it was too hard. The teacher asks:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'cat'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'meow'?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal name was easy to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal sound was hard to write today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The tracing and copying activities worked well for most students at this level, especially with the clear models. The peer labeling encouraged interaction and cross-checking, but some students needed significant teacher support to correctly identify and copy words from the board. Some students struggled with letter formation for 'Q' and 'R', which will require more dedicated practice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments needed: Provide more pre-printed labels for students who struggle with copying. Incorporate more multi-sensory approaches for letter formation (e.g., writing in sand, finger painting letters). Scaffold the collaborative labeling by pairing stronger writers with those who need more support.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their knowledge of animals and their sounds by creating a simple "Animal Sound Poster" or "Mini-Book" using their drawings and labels, integrating all four skills, and preparing for the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>